{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Three Legislative Steps in the U.S. Interstate Highway System","claim":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved three distinct legislative steps.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The title summarizes the three legislative functions described in the source and narrative.","materiality":"Frames the article's central historical sequence."},{"excerpt":"The legislation associated with the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct steps: studying a proposed toll network, authorizing designation of a national network, and providing for highway-construction funding.","claim":"The legislation involved three different functions: studying a proposed toll network, designating a national network, and funding construction.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This accurately synthesizes the separate purposes assigned to the 1938, 1944, and 1956 laws in the source.","materiality":"Establishes the article's central distinction between the laws."},{"excerpt":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the chief of the Bureau of Public Roads to study a proposed six-route toll network.","claim":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrative":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The date, law, official, and scope of the study match the source.","materiality":"Identifies the first legislative step and its subject."},{"excerpt":"The 1944 Act later authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","claim":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrative":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The act, authorization, mileage, and national-network scope match the source.","materiality":"The mileage and authorization distinguish this step from the earlier study."},{"excerpt":"It did not, by itself, complete that network.","claim":"The 1944 Act's authorization did not itself complete the national interstate network.","category":"outcome","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"Do not present authorization or designation as completion.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source describes the 1944 law as authorizing designation, not completing construction; the distinction is consistent with the later construction-funding provision.","materiality":"Prevents authorization from being mistaken for completion."},{"excerpt":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","claim":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions covering motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The funding purpose and listed tax subjects match the source.","materiality":"Describes the third legislative step and its provisions."},{"excerpt":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","claim":"The National Archives associates the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","category":"attribution","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrative":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The attribution is reproduced accurately; it is presented as the Archives' identification, not as a separate claim that the 1956 law completed the system.","materiality":"Provides an attributed characterization of the 1956 law."},{"excerpt":"The three legislative steps had different functions: feasibility study, network designation, and construction financing—not a single act of completion.","claim":"The three laws had distinct functions—feasibility study, network designation, and construction financing—and should not be treated as one act that completed the system.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The sequence distinguishes feasibility study, network designation, and a mechanism for construction financing; the National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This concluding synthesis reflects the separate functions stated in the source and does not claim that any single act completed the network.","materiality":"Reinforces the key distinction among study, designation, and construction financing."}]}

